  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is hotel management a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Hotel management is a real, growing career in India, not a dressed-up service job with no ceiling. But "is hotel
    management good" and "will the first three years feel good" are two different questions, and most articles on
    this topic blur them together until you cannot tell which one is actually being answered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the daily reality — rotating shifts, modest starting pay, physical stamina, and a
    documented industry-wide attrition problem — is genuinely demanding and not exaggerated. The payoff on the other
    side — department-head pay, GM-level pay, revenue-management specialisation, and real international mobility — is
    also genuinely real, but it belongs to people who build cross-department range and treat the early grind as
    skill-building, not a personal failure.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "hospitality is a glamorous global career, join now" pitch flooding institute brochures, and it
      is not the "never do hotel management, it will ruin your twenties" warning making rounds on career forums
      either. Both are incomplete. The field genuinely rewards the people who go in with accurate expectations and
      build real range — and genuinely burns out the people who went in on the lobby photo alone.
    </p>
  </div>

