            <td data-label="Real headroom">{row.headroom}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, aggregated from 2025-2026 salary-tracking platforms including PayScale and Indeed India,
    plus published hospitality-salary industry data, at the time of writing. Verify current figures against live
    listings for your specific city, property tier, and brand before anchoring a decision on any single number,
    including this one.
  </p>

  <p>
    So does hotel management have real growth headroom, or is it structurally capped? The honest answer: it is capped
    early and slow-moving in the middle, but it is not capped at the top. A generalist who never rotates departments
    or specialises genuinely plateaus in the mid-career range above.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ceiling that opens up — GM-level pay, luxury-chain compensation, or a fast-growing revenue-management track —
    belongs specifically to people who build cross-department range or add a commercial and data layer on top of
    operations, not to tenure alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scaling">How the biggest earners in hotel management actually scale</h2>

  <p>
    A generalist front-office or F&B role has a real, fairly low ceiling on its own. But the field has genuine
    headroom to scale toward significantly higher income and seniority for people who make a small number of
    specific moves — not a vague "work hard and you’ll rise" story.
