  <h2 id="attrition">The honest attrition and burnout numbers</h2>

  <p>
    This is the section most institute websites skip entirely, because it does not make for a good admissions pitch.
    It is also the single most useful thing to understand before you commit a year and real fees to this path.
  </p>

  <p>
    Industry studies place annual staff turnover in Indian hotels anywhere from roughly 40-50%, several times the
    10-15% average across industries generally — and some hotel-specific research puts the figure meaningfully
    higher still. The leading causes, according to hospitality HR research, are low starting pay, unclear growth
    timelines, work pressure, and better-paying opportunities outside the sector, not a lack of genuine interest in
    hospitality.
  </p>

  <p>
    In plain terms: a large share of people who leave are not leaving because the work itself was wrong for them.
    They are leaving because nobody told them how long the modest-pay, shift-heavy stretch would actually last.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      None of this means avoid hotel management. It means go in with accurate expectations. Graduates who know the
      first few years will be physically and socially demanding, and who treat that stretch as deliberate
      skill-building rather than a personal failure, consistently handle it better than graduates who expected an
      easier ride and quietly burn out inside the first two years.
    </p>
  </div>

